{"text": "Grant 1028-\u00a2\n\nGeorge Wolf, D.Phil.\n\nProfessor of Physiological Chemistry\nDepartment of Nutrition and Food Science\n\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\n77 Massachusetts Avenue\n\nActivation Dat\u00e9\n\nCambridge, Massachusetts 02139\n\nVitamin A and Glycoprotein Synthesis in Respiratory Epitheliuw, and Urinary\nGalactosyl Transferase, a Possible Early Marker Enzyme for Bladder Cancer.\n\nAim 1. Galactosylretinyl phosphate (GalRP) as an intermediate in tracheal\nsynthesis.\n(a) Completion of the confirmation that GalnP is synthesized by microsomal\nenzymes of trachea; evidence to be obtained that GalRP exists in trachea,\nbronchi and lungs in vivo; effect of vitamin A deficiency, repletion and\nexcess on tracheal and Tung GalRP.\n(b) Function of GalkP in trachea, Since the investigators detected synthesis\nof GalkP in trachea, it is likely that this substance would have a\nfunction in that tissue.\nIt is of importance then to establish whether GalRP transferase galactose to\noligolipids or to exogenous or endogenous glycoprotein acceptors, especially\nin view of the finding that retinyl phosphate stimulates uptake of galactose\nfrom UDP-gal into exogenous acceptor.\n\nAim 2, Galactosyl transferase in bladder cancer.\nA non-invasive test for the detection of bladder cancer. \u2018The researchers\nintend to investigate the possibility of using the ricinlectin (Fluorescent or\nbound to a dye) for detection of shed bladder cancer cells in human urine.\nThis aim is two-fold: (I) to find out conditions which will make the test as\napplied to bladder cancer cells in urine more reliable, comparable to the\nresponse given by biopsy specimens taken directly from the bladder; (II) to\nimprove the assay by making the lectin more highly fluorescent, or by attaching\na dye, so that a colorimetric test can be used. Once the test with urine works,\nit will be possible to screen heavy smokers for release of lectin-reacting\n\ncells in urine,\n\nFor basic research on the lectin response, the investigators are aiming to\ndetermine whether there is a causal connection or not between the increased\ngalactosyl transferase in the cell-surface of the carcinana cells, and the increased\nexposure of galactose.\n\nguly 1, 1980\n\nCurrent Grant Level: $38,890.00.\n\n6LSOTLSS:\n\nA~42D", "words": [{"text": "Grant", "x0": 550, "y0": 99, "x1": 585, "y1": 108, "conf": 82.0}, {"text": "1028-\u00a2", "x0": 592, "y0": 99, "x1": 633, "y1": 109, "conf": 53.0}, {"text": "George", "x0": 101, "y0": 139, "x1": 142, "y1": 150, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Wolf,", "x0": 149, "y0": 139, "x1": 182, "y1": 149, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "D.Phil.", "x0": 191, "y0": 139, "x1": 237, "y1": 149, "conf": 90.0}, {"text": "Professor", "x0": 101, "y0": 152, "x1": 163, "y1": 162, "conf": 93.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 170, "y0": 152, "x1": 183, "y1": 162, "conf": 89.0}, {"text": "Physiological", "x0": 191, "y0": 153, "x1": 280, "y1": 165, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "Chemistry", "x0": 286, "y0": 153, "x1": 350, "y1": 166, "conf": 91.0}, {"text": "Department", "x0": 101, "y0": 167, "x1": 170, "y1": 178, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 177, "y0": 166, "x1": 190, "y1": 176, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "Nutrition", "x0": 197, "y0": 167, "x1": 259, "y1": 176, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "and", "x0": 266, "y0": 167, "x1": 287, "y1": 176, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Food", "x0": 294, "y0": 168, "x1": 322, "y1": 176, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "Science", "x0": 328, "y0": 167, "x1": 377, "y1": 177, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "Massachusetts", "x0": 100, "y0": 180, "x1": 190, "y1": 190, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "Institute", "x0": 199, "y0": 181, "x1": 259, "y1": 190, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 266, "y0": 180, "x1": 280, "y1": 190, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Technology", "x0": 287, "y0": 181, "x1": 356, "y1": 193, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "77", "x0": 101, "y0": 194, "x1": 114, "y1": 203, "conf": 76.0}, {"text": "Massachusetts", "x0": 121, "y0": 194, "x1": 211, "y1": 204, "conf": 92.0}, {"tex